WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.220 --> 00:00:04.940
For people who don't like the cold,
winter is really miserable.

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:11.260
and here in the Arctic, it can be even more brutal.

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:14.480
But the Arctic and where you live, worlds apart,

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:16.940
may be more connected than you might think.

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:20.400
Scientists are finding that as
 temperatures in the Arctic rise,

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:22.260
changes here may be contributing to

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:25.720
extreme weather thousands of miles away.

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:34.080
Temperatures are rising across the world

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:36.840
but the warming is strongest in the Arctic.

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:39.920
Here the warming is twice as fast
as the global average.

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:41.800
And that's causing the ice to vanish.

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:46.460
In less than four decades, the Arctic 
has lost three-quarters of its sea ice.

00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:50.760
It covers a third less of the ocean than it did just 20 years ago.

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:53.300
That's drawn interest from an international group of researchers

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:57.220
who have come north to understand how the Arctic is rapidly changing.

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:05.720
These changes create a nasty feedback loop.

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:09.420
First the ice melts, turning a reflective surface into a dark one

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:12.160
that absorbs solar energy; warming up.

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:14.380
Without the ice, more water evaporates,

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:17.540
contributing to larger amounts of greenhouse gases.

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:24.640
And all that extra heat has had
surprising effects.

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:29.020
The process begins high in the atmosphere.

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:33.294
Earth's air tends to move from the warm middle of the planet to the poles.

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:36.120
Because warm air takes up more space - it's taller

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:39.860
so gravity pulls it towards the Arctic where the atmosphere is thinner and shorter

00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:41.900
and this creates wind.

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:46.540
The earth is spinning so instead a river of air known as the jet stream

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:48.540
is formed moving west to east.

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:50.560
This is what the polar jet stream looks like.

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:53.060
You can see the patterns of its winds.

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:55.960
The jet stream serves as a boundary between cold air from the Arctic

00:01:55.960 --> 00:01:57.960
and warm air from the tropics.

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:00.960
And its shape influences weather patterns.

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:05.380
The Arctic atmosphere is changing as the temperatures rise there faster than further south.

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:10.460
There is less gravitational pull on the air towards the North Pole, so the jet stream slows down.

00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:14.500
Less of a flow, and the jet stream is losing steam.

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:17.080
When it's curvy, weather sticks around longer

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:20.380
causing bigger storms, droughts, and cold spells.

00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:24.240
Some scientists think that's supercharging extreme weather across the world.

00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:28.660
From the Helmer Hanssen, I call atmospheric scientist Jennifer Francis

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:32.860
who explains how the polar jet stream affected the U.S. this past winter.

00:02:32.860 --> 00:02:35.820
FRANCIS: There was a huge northward
swing in the jet stream

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:39.440
over the west coast, bringing lots of warm air to Alaska.

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:41.920
NEWS ANCHOR: It's been the hottest December on record

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:45.240
with most temperatures 20 to 30 degrees above average.

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:48.260
FRANCIS: and then the jet stream took a big southward dive

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:52.440
over the Rockies and dipped way down into Florida.

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:57.360
NEWS ANCHOR: We haven't seen measurable snow in Tallahassee in over 30 years.

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:01.600
KINTISCH: It was so cold that alligators got frozen into a North Carolina swamp.

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:03.960
That stubborn pattern persisted for weeks

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:07.000
and the extreme cold and heat got worse and worse.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:10.640
NEWS ANCHOR: You can see why meteorologists are
calling it a bomb cyclone.

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:11.580
Bomb-

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:13.280
It's called Bombogenesis.

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:16.020
This is the current jet stream pattern. 
Does it look familiar?

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:19.900
It's the Arctic air driving so far south that it meets up with ocean air.

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:23.520
The Gulf Stream is warm humid air!

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:26.980
While on the research cruise, the jet stream sent the bomb cyclone

00:03:26.980 --> 00:03:30.140
up to the Arctic, and we got a wallop.

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:40.880
But then we enjoyed temperatures that
were almost ... pleasant.

00:03:42.340 --> 00:03:46.620
At times 20 degrees above average and warmer than those in New York City.

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:52.440
A week later, the jet stream brought extreme cold to Asia.

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:56.340
Russia and eastern China were pounded with frigid cold.

00:03:56.340 --> 00:04:00.280
Tokyo had its coldest day in 48 years.

00:04:00.280 --> 00:04:04.680
While scientists scramble to understand how the Arctic may be intensifying the weather,

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:07.400
some prominent climate researchers are skeptical.

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:10.120
Scientists can't definitively say whether any one

00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:12.540
weather event was caused by the warming Arctic,

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:15.320
conditions elsewhere, or by random chance.

00:04:15.320 --> 00:04:17.620
But Francis and others think the warming Arctic

00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:20.180
is loading the dice for extreme weather.

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:24.200
FRANCIS: We can confidently say that some amount of the increase

00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:27.140
in extreme weather that we're seeing, is because of climate change.

00:04:27.140 --> 00:04:29.560
This is not a question anymore.

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:33.900
KINTISCH: One thing is for sure, extreme weather in North America is occurring more often.

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:36.960
When the Arctic is very warm, very cold temperatures

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:40.360
in the eastern side of the United States are likely.

00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:46.200
Climate scientists have given this phenomenon a new name: warm arctic cold continents.

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:49.560
We tend to think of the Arctic as a victim of climate change,

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:51.820
a canary in a coal mine,

00:04:51.820 --> 00:04:54.980
but the vanishing ice may play a bigger role on the global stage,

00:04:54.980 --> 00:04:58.260
intensifying extreme weather a world away.

